Agent World is the control plane.

Remote Agents owns orchestration and governance.

The Coding Worker owns execution.

Model providers supply inference only.

GitHub remains the repository source of truth.

Core Principle

Models are not agents.

OpenRouter, OpenAI, Anthropic-compatible models, Gemini-compatible models and local models are interchangeable inference providers.

Remote Agents remains responsible for:

* agent identity
* sessions
* task state
* tool permissions
* execution routing
* approval gates
* evidence
* audit history
* repository context
* runtime state

Coding Session

A coding session should eventually contain:

* agent identity
* repository
* base branch
* working branch
* operator task
* selected model/provider
* assigned worker
* execution state
* tool activity
* changed files
* test results
* build results
* git diff
* approvals
* commit/PR state

Initial Tool Boundary

Prefer bounded tools instead of unrestricted shell access.

Initial conceptual tool surface:

repo.list
repo.read
repo.search
workspace.write
workspace.patch
tests.run
build.run
git.status
git.diff
git.commit
github.create_branch
github.create_pr

Shell execution, when required, must occur through an explicitly governed worker interface.

Do not expose arbitrary host/root execution directly to a model.

Browser Security Boundary

The browser must never require possession of:

* OpenRouter API keys
* OpenAI API keys
* GitHub private credentials
* SSH private keys
* worker credentials
* infrastructure secrets

Secrets remain server-side.

The browser receives only the authentication/session authority necessary to operate Agent World.

Approval Model

Repository mutation must remain observable.

The intended flow is:

TASK
  ↓
INSPECT
  ↓
PLAN
  ↓
EDIT
  ↓
TEST
  ↓
BUILD
  ↓
DIFF
  ↓
OPERATOR APPROVAL
  ↓
COMMIT / PR

High-impact mutations should fail closed when required identity, authority, routing or approval information is unresolved.

Initial Product Scope

Phase 1 — Repository Intelligence

* select repository
* inspect repository tree
* read/search files
* ask repository-aware questions
* stream agent activity
* model inference through adapter

No autonomous mutation required.

Phase 2 — Governed Editing

* isolated working copy
* patch generation
* changed-file tracking
* diff viewer
* explicit approval
* controlled commit

Phase 3 — Coding Runtime

* bounded tool loop
* test execution
* build execution
* retry/fix cycles
* branches
* pull requests
* persistent session history

Phase 4 — Distributed Remote Agents

* multiple execution workers
* Windows worker
* Linux/container worker
* optional GPU/local-model worker
* capability-aware routing
* model-provider selection
* deeper Sentient OS governance

UI Direction

Do not recreate a full IDE.

Agent World remains an operational control plane.

The dominant interaction should be:

1. select agent
2. select repository/context
3. issue task
4. observe plan and execution
5. inspect evidence and changes
6. approve or reject mutations

File viewing and diff inspection support this workflow but should not turn Agent World into a VS Code clone.

Compatibility

The architecture must remain compatible with:

* existing Agent World read models
* Remote Agents
* Sentient OS governance
* GitHub
* OpenRouter
* direct model APIs
* Ollama/local inference
* Windows workers
* Linux workers
* future isolated/container runtimes

Definition of Success

From a browser, an authorized operator can eventually:

1. open Agent World;
2. select a coding agent;
3. select an authorized GitHub repository;
4. issue a development task;
5. observe repository inspection and execution;
6. inspect resulting changes and validation evidence;
7. approve the mutation;
8. create a controlled commit or pull request;

without requiring a development environment on the browser client.

This document defines the intended architecture unless explicitly superseded by a later approved architecture decision.